# Rust build output, for every crate here
**/target/
**/*.rlib
**/*.working

# A lock file is machine state, not repository content: it is generated here and
# by any CI job that needs one, and it is never committed - in this repository or
# any other. This line is what makes that true for a clone that has no global
# ignore of its own, and it is why no workflow step passes `--locked`.
Cargo.lock

# Local run artefacts: frames, traces and probe output from this machine.
# Named one by one rather than with *.json, because that also swallowed
# Schema/*.json - which is the repository's data, not this machine's.
*.jpg
*.png
*.jsonl
cuecorr.json
postest.json
memread.json
memscan.json
state.json

# Working captures: a research transcript is evidence that a conversation
# happened, not repository content. The files stay on disk - they are read while
# a decision is being made - and what a capture settles is written into
# Documentation/ as prose.
Documentation/Research/

# Node
node_modules/

# The game's own source, cloned for reference (not part of this repository)
Stem/

# Credentials: the names travel in App/.env.example, the values never do.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example

# Editor and OS
.DS_Store
.idea/
.vscode/
